<DocsLayout
  title="Quickstart"
  subtitle="From workspace dependencies to a desktop window backed by the real local sidecar."
  description="Install and run CADCAMFUN desktop, distinguish preview mode, locate data, and verify a checkout."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="before" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <p>The workspace declares Bun <code>1.4.4</code> as its package manager. Use the repository lockfile for dependency installation. Native desktop development also requires a Rust toolchain, the platform's Tauri build dependencies, and an installed system webview. The Tauri CLI is already a desktop development dependency.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>macOS:</strong> Xcode command-line tools for Rust/native linking and packaging.</li>
      <li><strong>Windows:</strong> the MSVC toolchain and WebView2 runtime/build prerequisites.</li>
      <li><strong>Linux:</strong> WebKitGTK 4.1, application-indicator, librsvg, OpenSSL, and bundler tools. The CI workflow records the packages used on Ubuntu.</li>
      <li><strong>iOS companion:</strong> macOS, Xcode, CocoaPods, and a native Expo build containing the local pinning module.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A browser preview requires Bun and workspace dependencies but does not compile the Rust shell. A release installer additionally requires signing configuration; that is separate from local UI development.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Install dependencies</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="From the repository root">
{`bun install
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop typecheck
bun test packages/core`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Install once at the workspace root so local <code>workspace:*</code> dependencies resolve together. CI uses <code>bun install --frozen-lockfile</code> to reject lockfile drift. Use the package scripts to select the repository's configured compiler rather than assuming every package uses the same TypeScript executable.</p>
    <p>The targeted commands above provide a quick source check. The full workspace checks are <code>bun run typecheck</code> and <code>bun run test</code>; they cover more modules and may take longer. An individual passing package does not prove all runtime integrations are configured.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="migrate" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Database setup</h2>
    <p>The real desktop does not require a separate CLI migration command. Its launcher supplies the application data directory and staged migrations to the sidecar; opening the database applies pending migrations. <code>bun run desktop:dev</code> stages the migration resource as part of preparing the sidecar.</p>
    <p>The database command-line tool is separate. <code>bun run db:migrate</code> delegates to the database package and defaults to <code>./data/cadcamfun.db</code> relative to that package's process directory, normally <code>packages/db/data/cadcamfun.db</code> when invoked through the root script. Set an absolute <code>DATABASE_URL</code> when you need to target a specific database.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Only when changing the database schema">
{`bun run db:generate
bun run db:migrate`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Generation reads <code>packages/db/src/schema.ts</code> and writes migrations under <code>packages/db/drizzle</code>. Applying migrations updates the selected CLI database, not every application database. Native desktop data lives in the Tauri application data directory; a manually started sidecar uses <code>CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR</code>.</p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Choose the database deliberately">
      <p>A successful CLI migration does not change which database the desktop opens. Locate the sidecar data directory before investigating missing projects or applying a manual data operation.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="desktop" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Open the desktop</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="From the repository root">
{`bun run desktop:dev`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>This compiles the real Bun sidecar and stages migrations, then runs Tauri. Tauri starts Vite at <code>http://localhost:1420</code> and opens the webview. The Rust shell waits for the sidecar's ready handshake; the webview checks health before showing routed screens.</p>
    <p>Keep the launching terminal open. The shell owns the sidecar lifecycle and supplies its launch token and paths. If startup fails, inspect the boot reason and application logs. The webview retries endpoint discovery for up to 150 seconds; the shell permits one automatic sidecar restart.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Browser-only UI preview">
{`bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop dev`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Open <code>http://localhost:1420</code>. With no <code>VITE_SIDECAR_URL</code>, this uses the in-memory fake API. It supports a subset of requests and does not provide persistent projects, provider sessions, or native dialogs. Connect to a live sidecar by setting both Vite sidecar URL and token process variables.</p>
    <p>The desktop package's <code>tauri:dev</code> script creates a stub sidecar when the binary is missing. The stub supports shell isolation. Prefer the root <code>desktop:dev</code> command when exercising application services.</p>
    <p><code>bun run desktop:dev</code> first runs <code>build:sidecar</code>, which compiles the sidecar and stages migrations (pipeline steps <code>compile</code> and <code>resources</code>). It then starts <code>tauri dev</code> in the desktop package.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="after" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Verify the first session</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Wait for the application to leave the boot screen and confirm it is connected to the intended API.</li>
      <li>Create or open a project and enter CAD or CAM.</li>
      <li>Save the work and reopen it through the project/resource UI to verify persistence.</li>
      <li>If using the assistant, configure a supported model provider in desktop settings before starting a conversation.</li>
      <li>If using the iOS companion, enable its gateway and create a device pairing on the desktop.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Testing a visible path does not validate machining safety. Inspect stock, tooling, machine limits, post output, and verification results for the actual operation before transferring NC output to a machine.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Run the documentation site">
{`bun run --cwd packages/web start
bun run --cwd packages/web check
bun run --cwd packages/web build`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The web <code>start</code> command runs Astro. Use its printed local address. The web <code>dev</code> script additionally starts the Studio API, which can open the selected local database; use it when working on Studio rather than static documentation alone.</p>
  </section>

