  <section id="project-smoke" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Verify a real API without a model</h2>
    <p>Start the manual sidecar described in the local development guide, or use the endpoint/token of a development process you control. The following recipe creates one disposable project and lists it. It needs curl; the optional identifier extraction uses Bun. Replace the example port and token with those of your isolated sidecar.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Second terminal: health, create, list, read">
{`CADCAM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:43111
CADCAM_TOKEN=local-development-token

curl --fail-with-body "$CADCAM_URL/api/health"

curl --fail-with-body -X POST "$CADCAM_URL/api/projects" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $CADCAM_TOKEN" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"name":"Documentation smoke","description":"Disposable local check","isPublic":false}' \
  -o /tmp/cadcamfun-project-smoke.json

curl --fail-with-body "$CADCAM_URL/api/projects" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $CADCAM_TOKEN"

CADCAM_PROJECT_ID=$(bun --no-env-file -e \
  'const row = await Bun.file("/tmp/cadcamfun-project-smoke.json").json(); console.write(row.id)')
curl --fail-with-body "$CADCAM_URL/api/projects/$CADCAM_PROJECT_ID" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $CADCAM_TOKEN"`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Health returns a status/timestamp object. Project creation returns HTTP 201 and a project row with its identifier. Listing returns an array of projects with owner/organization relations and counts; reading by identifier returns one such related project. The example does not call a model or generate machine output.</p>
    <p>Close the manual sidecar cleanly, restart it with the same data directory, update the ephemeral URL, and list projects again to check persistence. A new launch may choose a different port. If this project is absent, first compare the data directory, client endpoint, and authorization context.</p>
    <p>Cleanup is optional and destructive for this one disposable project: send <code>DELETE /api/projects/&lt;id&gt;</code> only after confirming the returned identifier. Creation/list/read exercise application persistence without requiring cleanup to succeed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="first-run-errors" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Interpret first-run failures</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Likely boundary</th><th>Next action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Dependency install cannot resolve a local package</td><td>Workspace installation</td><td>Run from the repository root with the declared Bun version and lockfile.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Native linking or WebKit error</td><td>Tauri prerequisites</td><td>Install the toolchain/platform libraries used by the matching CI target.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No ready event</td><td>Sidecar configuration, migration, or startup</td><td>Inspect stderr and the structured error event. Keep stdin open.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Health works, project call returns 401</td><td>Authorization</td><td>Health is public; protected API calls still need the current process token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Create returns 400</td><td>Request validation/business validation</td><td>Send JSON with a nonempty name and inspect the returned message.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Create returns 403 with organization data</td><td>Organization membership</td><td>Use an accessible organization or omit the organization field for a local check.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Native companion says pinning is unavailable</td><td>Missing local Expo module</td><td>Use a custom iOS build, not Expo Go.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Keep the first verification small: health, one project, one persisted drawing/program, then optional integrations. A successful base application launch does not configure Stripe, AI providers, a remote GitHub repository, or mobile routing automatically.</p>
  </section>

